{"name":"Meteor Dust","hex":"#6e6970","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"meteor","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An atmospheric or meteorological phenomenon. These were sometimes classified as aerial or airy meteors (winds), aqueous or watery meteors (hydrometeors: clouds, rain, snow, hail, dew, frost), luminous meteors (rainbows and aurora), and igneous or fiery meteors (lightning and shooting stars). .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A fast-moving streak of light in the night sky caused by the entry of extraterrestrial matter into the earth's atmosphere; a shooting star or falling star."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move at great speed."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/meteor"},{"term":"dust","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Fine particles.","The act of cleaning by dusting."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To remove dust from.","To remove dust; to clean by removing dust."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dust"}],"entries":[{"title":"Meteor","blocks":[{"text":"A meteor, known colloquially as a shooting star, is a glowing streak of a small body going through Earth's atmosphere, after being heated to incandescence by collisions with air molecules in the upper atmosphere, creating a streak of light via its rapid motion and sometimes also by shedding glowing material in its wake. Meteors typically occur in the mesosphere at altitudes from 76–100 kilometres. The root word meteor comes from the Greek μετεωρίτης (meteōrítēs), meaning \"high in the air\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg/330px-Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meteor","wikidata":"Q131136"},{"title":"Dust","blocks":[{"text":"Dust is made of fine particles of solid matter. On Earth, it generally consists of particles in the atmosphere that come from various sources such as soil lifted by wind, volcanic eruptions, and pollution."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Dust_storm_approaching_Stratford%2C_Texas.jpg/330px-Dust_storm_approaching_Stratford%2C_Texas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dust","wikidata":"Q165632"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg/500px-Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg/1280px-Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg/500px-Cosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Meteor seen from the site of the Atacama Large Millimeter Array (ALMA)[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACosmic_Fireball_Falling_Over_ALMA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif/500px-P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif/500px-P10220XX.01.49.MESZ.14.08.2019.Berlin.Perseiden.x1.0.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A meteoroid of the Perseids with a size of about 1 cm (3⁄8 in) entering the earth's atmosphere in real time. The meteoroid is at the bright head of the trail, and the ionisation of the mesosphere is still visible in the tail.","link":"